  pages={1-5},
  abstract={Achieving reliable Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) demands architectures that merge the stability of symbolic reasoning with the adaptability of neural networks. This work introduces a novel neuro-symbolic AGI framework that integrates hybrid model design specifically, knowledge fusion through a neural-symbolic attention mechanism with formal verification to enforce symbolic constraints. We analyze the limitations of purely neural and purely symbolic paradigms, benchmarking prior models such as DeepProbLog and Logic Tensor Networks against our approach. The proposed dynamic integration employs graph networks for relational reasoning and is validated through an ethical decision-making case study illustrating interpretable rule execution. Experimental results on AGI-scale tasks, including question answering and multiagent planning, reveal a 25% improvement in adversarial robustness and a 40% gain in explainability (via SHAP and logic-based audits). The framework advances scalable, verifiable AGI engineering with explicit attention to fairness, safety, and human-AI collaboration.},
